import { Meta, ColorPalette, ColorItem } from '@storybook/addon-docs/blocks';
import { CopyButton } from '../src/lib/components/buttonv2/CopyButton.component.tsx';
import { coreUIAvailableThemes } from '../src/lib/style/theme';

<Meta title="Style/Colors" />

# Colors

## Using a colour

Every colour comes from the palette below. Pass a token name to a prop that takes a
colour on its own — `color`, `backgroundColor`, `borderColor` — or read the token off
the theme inside a styled component:

```jsx
<Box
  color="textPrimary"
  backgroundColor="backgroundLevel1"
  borderColor="border"
/>
```

```js
const Alert = styled.div`
  color: ${(props) => props.theme.statusCritical};
  border: 1px solid ${(props) => props.theme.border};
`;
```

These props accept a token name and nothing else, so a value outside the palette fails
to compile and a near miss is corrected for you (`statusHealty` gives _Did you mean
`statusHealthy`?_). Three CSS keywords are also accepted, because they defer to the
context rather than naming a colour: `currentColor`, `inherit` and `transparent`.

Passing a token's **value** as a prop is rejected too, since its type is `string`:

```jsx
<Box color={theme.statusCritical} /> // does not compile
<Box color="statusCritical" /> // the same colour, and checkable
```

Both render identically and both follow a theme switch — the second is the one the
compiler can check, so prefer it.

A prop that bundles a colour in among other values stays raw CSS. Name the colour
through its own prop to have it checked.

### If you need a colour outside the palette

A colour outside the palette cannot follow a theme switch, so it drifts from the rest
of the product as soon as the theme changes. Stay on the tokens unless the value
genuinely sits outside the design system — a one-off illustration, a third-party brand
colour.

For those, use `styled()`. The CSS inside it is not type-checked, so any value the
browser accepts works, and it overrides the component's own styles — including a token
passed as a prop on the same element:

```js
const Illustration = styled(Box)`
  color: #e40046;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, #fff, #000);
`;
```

For a gradient or an image on a `Box`, `backgroundImage` also takes raw CSS.

